When I was in high school, we had this big integrative subject where we were supposed to work on a project throughout the entire year. The idea was that the project would bring together everything we had learned. But honestly, my group and I didn’t do anything all year. We just kept postponing it.

Then, three days before the presentation, we finally panicked and got to work. We decided to build a model of a car wash. The concept was that as the car moved along the conveyor belt, sensors would detect it and trigger different processes step by step.

Those three days were brutal—we barely slept at all, staying up late every night to finish the model. In the end, it actually turned out pretty good. From the outside, it looked neat and worked fine. But if you looked underneath, there were tons of tangled wires—it was a complete mess. Luckily, that part wasn’t visible during the presentation.

We managed to pull it off, gave our presentation, and to our surprise, our project was a success!